WriteArchiveAscii::write_mat3x3 emits a `rawFloat:` entry that neither
ReadArchiveAscii nor ZenGin can read.

`ReadArchiveAscii::read_mat3x3` reads an entry typed `raw` and parses it as 36
hex-encoded bytes; ZenGin writes `raw` too - all 1277 `trafoOSToWSRot` entries
across a retail Gothic II install's ASCII worlds are `raw:`. The writer emits
`rawFloat:` with the nine floats in decimal, which `read_entry("raw")` rejects
outright: ZenKit cannot read back its own output. The same class of
writer/reader disagreement as 0024 and 0026.

`write_mat3x3` has exactly one caller in the library - `VirtualObject::save`'s
unpacked branch - which is why no retail byte and no fixture changed until the
unpacked path became reachable (see 0046). The element order is unchanged: the
writer transposes and writes row-major, and `read_mat3x3` fills row-major and
transposes back.

Upstreamable as-is: a self-evident writer/reader disagreement, no API change.

--- a/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
@@ -365,8 +365,12 @@
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveAscii::write_mat3x3(std::string_view name, Mat3 const& v) {
+		// A `raw:` hex entry, not `rawFloat:`: ReadArchiveAscii::read_mat3x3 reads
+		// `raw` and ZenGin writes `raw`, so a `rawFloat` entry is one neither can
+		// read back. The transpose and the element order are read_mat3x3's own,
+		// which transposes again after filling row-major.
 		auto vT = v.transpose();
-		this->write_raw_float(name, vT.pointer(), 9);
+		this->write_raw(name, reinterpret_cast<std::byte const*>(vT.pointer()), 9 * sizeof(float));
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveAscii::write_raw(std::string_view name, std::vector<std::byte> const& v) {
